Venetium 0019 - sandbox interception for Windows ARM32 (Thumb-2): resolvers, interceptors and the wiring around them.

BATCH-ARM-1 section 3C (plan group sandbox-arm32-0019, decision 5). Chromium 150 has no ARM32 sandbox interception: sandbox/win has
x86 (stdcall, injected first argument), x64 and arm64 (g_originals table) back ends and nothing for current_cpu "arm". Written from 150's
own siblings (resolver_64.cc / service_resolver_64.cc / interceptors_64.*), not from CR109. 13 files: 4 new, 9 edited.

New:
- sandbox/win/src/resolver_arm.cc: ResolverThunk's internal thunk, 8 bytes of Thumb-2: ldr.w pc, [pc, #0] (DF F8 00 F0) + a 4-byte literal
  (the interceptor; bit 0 forced to 1). It fits exactly in an ntdll system call stub and only modifies pc.
- sandbox/win/src/service_resolver_arm.cc: ServiceResolverThunk for the ARM32 ntdll Nt* stubs. MEASURED on the Surface 2's ntdll.dll
  (10.0.15035): 456 of 457 Nt* exports are mov.w r12,#id (356) or movw r12,#id16 (100), svc #1, bx lr (8 bytes), 16-byte stride, 8 zero
  bytes of padding after, every export RVA has bit 0 set (NtGetTickCount is the one that is not a stub). The resolver verifies exactly that
  shape (both mov forms; Rd = r12, S = 0), requires a 4-byte aligned site (the literal load aligns pc down), strips the Thumb bit from the
  resolved address before Read/WriteProcessMemory, and saves the 8 original bytes (callable: mov, svc, bx lr are position independent).
  No Wow64/Win8 classes (native ARM32). No instruction-cache flush: marked in PerformPatch where one would go (deferred to the device run).
- sandbox/win/src/interceptors_arm.h / interceptors_arm.cc: the 19 interceptors with the "Arm" suffix, mechanically derived from
  interceptors_64.* (the AAPCS cannot receive the x86 injected first argument; the original comes from g_originals by InterceptorId).

Edited:
- sandbox/win/BUILD.gn: current_cpu "arm" sources for :service_resolver and :sandbox.
- sandbox/win/src/interceptors.h, interception.h (MAKE_SERVICE_NAME suffix Arm).
- sandbox/win/src/interception.cc: Supermium's x86-only resolver selection (Win8ResolverThunk, Wow64*ResolverThunk: x86 byte patterns,
  IsFunctionAService defined only in service_resolver_32.cc) is not taken on ARM32 (a plain ServiceResolverThunk, as on _WIN64); the
  g_originals entry of the parent is stored with bit 0 set (the thunk is Thumb code called through a pointer).
- sandbox/win/src/interception_agent.cc: the same bit 0 for the child's (EAT) g_originals entries.
- sandbox/win/src/eat_resolver.cc: the two-thunk form (return path + forward path) as on x64/arm64, and bit 0 on the patched export RVA.
- sandbox/win/src/sandbox_nt_util.h: the _InterlockedCompareExchange[Pointer] intrinsics for _M_ARM (was #error Architecture not supported).
- sandbox/win/src/process_mitigations.cc: #error This platform is not supported -> _M_ARM allowed in ConvertProcessMitigationsToPolicy;
  and DEP/SEHOP treated as on 64-bit (always on / not applicable on ARM: NX is mandatory, SEH is table based): no SetProcessDEPPolicy call
  and no PROCESS_CREATION_MITIGATION_POLICY_DEP/SEHOP flags on ARM32. (Judgment call: the two hunks at the DEP/SEHOP #if lines can be
  dropped to compile them in instead; see the results.)
- chrome/chrome_elf/third_party_dlls/hook.cc: the second consumer of ServiceResolverThunk uses the table-style wrapper
  (NewNtMapViewOfSection64) on ARM32, with bit 0 on the original thunk address.

Checked (STRUCTURAL/RUNTIME, see the results): all 49 :sandbox/:service_resolver sources + the 3 new + chrome_elf hook.cc compile for
ARMNT (/WX) with the patch (before it: 30 of 49 fail on the two #errors); the symbol closure of the 52 objects is complete for the
interception symbols; the stub recognition and patch logic of the real new files run against the real Surface 2 stub bytes (927 checks).
Device-only: the patch actually running, instruction-cache behaviour, CFG on the thunks, SEH under a real fault, _HEAP_32 / kShimDataOffset.
Lead check in the real arm-rt21 graph (RUNTIME, after gn gen): the sandbox targets and chrome_elf hook.obj build as ARMNT objects.

